--TEST--
Core\Math::mod throws on a zero divisor where fmod answers NAN, and it is the float half of a split fmod does not make
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
Two divergences, and both come from `Core\Math::mod`'s signature and its one guard rather than from its arithmetic — every row of `math-int-div-and-mod-match-intdiv-and-fmod` agrees, `NAN` and signed-zero rows included. **The zero divisor.** `fmod($a, 0.0)` is `NAN` in PHP, for any dividend; `Core\Math::mod` throws. The distinction the member's doc comment draws is between a *domain* error, which stays at IEEE's answer — an infinite dividend, a `NAN` operand, both asserted as agreeing rows in the matching case — and a *division by zero*, which spec § 3 makes a throw wherever it appears, so `mod` refuses it exactly as `intDiv` and the `%` operator do. Answering `NAN` instead would make the one arithmetic failure the language has a chance of catching indistinguishable from an ordinary result, and `NAN` propagates silently through every subsequent operation. **The split.** PHP's `fmod` takes two `int`s and widens them, so it covers the integer remainder as well; `Core\Math::mod` is declared `float, float` and `Core\Math::mod(7, 2)` is `E0401` — ADR 0007 § 2 has no implicit widening, so the integer remainder is the `%` operator and nothing else. The two spellings answer the same values, which is what the last two rows here show and what the matching case's counted identity asserts across a whole table; what differs is that the choice is made at compile time rather than by the argument types at the call. `%` and `intDiv` agree with `mod` about a zero divisor, so the refusal belongs to the language and not to this member.
--FILE--
<?mwl
// A zero divisor, on the three dividends that would each reach `NAN` by a
// different route in PHP: an ordinary value, a zero, and an infinity. All
// three are one refusal here, which is the point — the divisor is what is
// wrong, and the dividend takes no part in deciding that.
try {
    var $ordinary = Core\Math::mod(7.5, 0.0);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $byZero) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

try {
    var $zeroOverZero = Core\Math::mod(0.0, 0.0);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $zeroDividend) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

float $huge = Core\Math::INFINITY;
try {
    var $infiniteOverZero = Core\Math::mod($huge, 0.0);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $infiniteDividend) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

// A negative zero divisor is the same refusal: the guard is `$b == 0.0`, which
// IEEE makes true of both zeros, so there is no divisor that is zero for one
// sign and not for the other.
try {
    var $negativeZero = Core\Math::mod(7.5, -0.0);
    echo "answered\n";
} catch (Throwable $signedZero) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

// The integer half of the split, which PHP reaches through the same `fmod`.
// `Core\Math::mod(7, 2)` does not compile — both parameters are `float` and
// ADR 0007 § 2 has no implicit widening — so `%` is the spelling, and its
// answers are the ones `fmod` gives for the same pair.
echo 7 % 2, " ", -7 % 2, " ", 7 % -2, "\n";

// And `%` refuses a zero divisor exactly as `mod` does, which is what makes
// the refusal a property of the language rather than of one member. PHP's `%`
// throws here too; it is only `fmod` that answers.
try {
    int $undefined = 7 % 0;
    echo "divided\n";
} catch (Throwable $intByZero) {
    echo "refused\n";
}

// The half that does not diverge, on the same member: any non-zero divisor.
echo Core\Math::mod(7.5, 2.0), " ", Core\Math::mod(-7.5, 2.0), "\n";
--EXPECT--
refused
refused
refused
refused
1 -1 1
refused
1.5 -1.5
